diff --git a/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md b/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
index a4f48b5..642d20b 100644
--- a/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
+++ b/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
@@ -159,3 +159,43 @@ __Response:__
 ```
 
 `request` and `response` can be `null` for deliveries that have not started yet or are still in progress. The delivery request headers omit the `X-Webhook-Signature` header.
+
+## Deliveries
+
+When a subscribed event happens on the board, Fizzy posts it to the webhook's payload URL in the background and logs the attempt as a delivery.
+
+- Each event is delivered to each subscribed webhook exactly once. Deliveries are claimed before they are posted, so a retried job never posts the same event twice.
+- Regular receivers get the event as JSON with `Content-Type: application/json`. Slack incoming webhooks get `{"text": "..."}`, and Campfire and Basecamp chatbot URLs get an HTML message with `Content-Type: text/html`.
+- Every request carries these headers:
+
+```
+User-Agent: fizzy/1.0.0 Webhook
+Content-Type: application/json
+X-Webhook-Timestamp: 2025-12-05T19:36:35.401Z
+X-Webhook-Signature: 12ab…
+```
+
+`X-Webhook-Timestamp` is when the event happened. `X-Webhook-Signature` is the hex-encoded `HMAC-SHA256` of the request body, keyed with the webhook's `signing_secret`, so receivers can verify the request came from Fizzy:
+
+```ruby
+OpenSSL::HMAC.hexdigest("SHA256", signing_secret, request.raw_post) == request.headers["X-Webhook-Signature"]
+```
+
+__Example JSON payload:__
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "03f5v9zkft4hj9qq0lsn9ohde",
+  "action": "card_closed",
+  "created_at": "2025-12-05T19:36:35.401Z",
+  "eventable": { "id": "03f6card042", "number": 42, "title": "Fix mobile login", "…": "…" },
+  "board": { "id": "03f5v9zkft4hj9qq0lsn9ohcy", "name": "Fizzy", "…": "…" },
+  "creator": { "id": "03f5v9zjw7pz8717a4no1h8a7", "name": "David Heinemeier Hansson", "…": "…" }
+}
+```
+
+A delivery is `completed` once the receiver answers, whatever it answers, and `errored` when the receiver can't be reached at all. Anything outside a `2XX` response counts as a failure.
+
+Receivers that keep failing are switched off: after 10 failed deliveries in a row within an hour, the webhook is deactivated and stops receiving events until it's reactivated with `POST .../activation`. A successful delivery clears the count.
+
+Deliveries are kept for a week and then deleted.
